ck": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Select the correct answer."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le01ob02re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob02re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>: I'm not going out tonight, I have to bring this work forward. <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:  What are you doing? <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  I'm sorting the requirements of my project out.  <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:  You aren't doing very well. You have a bunch of requirement types over there.  <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:   Well, that's because there are many types of requirements in system engineering.<br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>: Really? Why?<br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  Requirements encompass needs and statements of different kinds. They identify particular attributes or capabilities of a system. So, the information is massive.<br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:   I understand. <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  I don't intent to narrow down the requirements into small groups because that's impossible! For now,  I just need to sort out how to organize the functional, architectural, structural, and behavioral requirements; but it's complicated because there are also non-functional, performance, design, derived, allocated, and customers' requirements."}, {"time": "58000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob02re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:  That's a lot! What's the difference between non-functional and functional? <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  Well, functional requirements are used as the top-level functions. They identify tasks to be accomplished; while non-functional specifies criteria to judge not the functions, but the operation of a system. <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:  And, what do customers' requirements do? <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  Those are statements of fact and assumptions that see about expectations of a system. Those expectations are mission, objectives, environment, and constraints. Customer requirements help us estimate the system's suitability and effectiveness."}, {"time": "99000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:   And what about the other requirements? What are they for?<br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Tom</span>:  Design requirements are the technical side of the system, they specify the building, coding, and executing purposes of the system. The structural requirements define the task to perform in relation to the structure of the system. And, the behavioral ones identify the system behavior or performance. Sweetie, I don't have time to talk over this now, I need to get this done. <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Kelly</span>:  OK. I'm not going to interrupt you anymore. Thanks for the explanation. Bye!"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le01ob02re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}